            ["When rain falls on sloped ground, most of it does not soak in. It sheets downhill, gathers into rivulets, then into channels, and leaves the property within hours.","When rainfall exceeds the ground’s infiltration capacity on sloped land, some water moves downslope as runoff. It can gather into rivulets and channels and leave the site quickly."],
            ["In late summer, there is too little. A stream between rainstorms is not fed by rain. It is fed by groundwater slowly seeping into the channel from the surrounding land — a flow called baseflow. Land that sheds its rain quickly has little stored to give back. The well runs low, the brook runs thin, and the pasture browns first.","During dry periods, streamflow is often sustained by groundwater and other delayed subsurface contributions called baseflow. Landscapes that store less water have less available to release between storms, which can contribute to low streamflow, dry soils, and stressed wells."],
