            ["A bioswale is a shallow, planted trench dug along the contour of a slope — level from end to end, so water cannot run along it.","In Earthline’s use, a bioswale is a shallow, planted contour trench intended to interrupt downslope runoff and spread it along a nearly level alignment."],
            ["Water running downhill meets the trench and stops. Held there, it does the one thing runoff never does: it waits. And while it waits, it soaks downward through the soil.","Runoff reaching the swale slows and can temporarily pond. The added residence time creates an opportunity for infiltration instead of immediate downslope conveyance."],
            ["Sink it. Standing water infiltrates. It passes down through soil and rock and joins the groundwater below — the saturated zone that feeds wells and springs.","Sink it. Ponded water has time to infiltrate. Some remains in the soil and root zone; some may move deeper and, where local hydrogeology allows, contribute to groundwater recharge."],
            ["This is old technology. Contour water-harvesting earthworks appear independently in Andean, South Asian, North African, and Southwestern Indigenous agriculture, in some cases for thousands of years. Earthline did not invent this method. It locates where the method applies.","Contour water-harvesting has long precedents in Andean, South Asian, North African, and Southwestern Indigenous land-management traditions. Earthline did not invent the method; it screens landscapes for places where the underlying hydrologic principle may be useful."],
            ["Water that infiltrates does not stay under the swale. It moves — slowly, underground, downhill — along paths that can run for hundreds of meters or many kilometres, and it eventually re-emerges. It surfaces in springs, in wetlands, and in stream channels, where it becomes the baseflow that keeps water moving in dry weather.","Infiltrated water can remain in the root zone, move laterally through shallow subsurface layers, or travel deeper along groundwater flow paths. Where those paths connect to springs, wetlands, or streams, part of that water can later contribute to baseflow."],
            ["The transit is slow by design. Groundwater on longer, deeper paths spends more time in the subsurface than groundwater on short shallow ones. That delay is the entire benefit: rain that would have left in six hours instead leaves over weeks or months, arriving when the landscape needs it rather than when it can least use it.","Longer subsurface pathways can increase residence time. That delay is one major benefit of infiltration-based retention: some water that might have become rapid runoff can instead remain in the landscape and be released more slowly."],
            ["Water intercepted near the bottom of a slope has almost no landscape left to travel through. It infiltrates and re-emerges a short distance later.","Water intercepted low on a slope may have a shorter subsurface path before reaching a discharge area, depending on local geology and groundwater gradients."],
            ["Water intercepted higher up enters a longer flow path. It travels farther underground, stays stored longer, and re-emerges further down the system — supporting soil moisture, springs, wells, and stream baseflow across everything between the recharge point and the discharge point.","In many hillslope settings, water infiltrated higher on a slope can enter longer subsurface pathways and remain stored longer before discharge. The actual path and travel time depend on soils, bedrock, fractures, and groundwater gradients."],
            ["A bioswale is not an addition to a natural system. It is a partial replacement for retention that was industrially removed from this watershed roughly four centuries ago and never restored. The comparison is not intervention versus untouched nature. It is intervention versus a landscape already stripped of most of its capacity to hold water.","The northeastern beaver record is a historical example of how land-use change can remove distributed water storage. A bioswale does not recreate a beaver wetland; it uses the same broad principle of slowing water and increasing residence time on appropriate hillslopes."],
            ["Beavers held the channels. Deep forest soils and their organic layers held the hillslopes. Trapping removed the first; clearing, grazing, and compaction degraded the second. Bioswales address the hillslope half — and they rebuild it, because a swale re-waters ground where native trees and vegetation can be planted back, that vegetation drops organic material that becomes mulch and then new soil, and roots and berm together retain the soil that would otherwise be washed downstream.","In forested landscapes, channel storage, deep soils, organic layers, and root networks all contribute to slowing and storing water. On suitable hillslopes, planted swales can complement those functions by retaining runoff, supporting vegetation, and reducing soil loss."],
            ["Groundwater recharge is why Earthline ranks a corridor. It is not the only thing the corridor does.","Groundwater-recharge potential is a central consideration in Earthline’s corridor ranking, but it is not the only landscape function that matters."],
            ["Cuts the flood peak. Retention structures reduced peak flows by up to 74% in monitored field studies — the water still arrives, just later and lower.","Can reduce peak runoff under appropriate conditions. Related monitored bioretention systems have reported peak-flow reductions up to 74%, but those values are not a performance prediction for a contour swale."],
            ["Recharges groundwater — the wells, springs, and dry-season stream flow that a landscape lives on.","Creates opportunities for infiltration and, where hydrogeology permits, groundwater recharge that can support wells, springs, and dry-season streamflow."],
